WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.667
What METR reported in 2026.

2
00:00:02.947 --> 00:00:06.851
Three updates matter, and each comes with a caveat from METR itself.

3
00:00:07.131 --> 00:00:08.411
Time Horizon 1.

4
00:00:08.691 --> 00:00:11.869
1 (29 January 2026).

5
00:00:12.149 --> 00:00:22.517
METR enlarged its suite from 170 to 228 tasks, added more long tasks, and moved to the open-source Inspect evaluation framework.

6
00:00:22.797 --> 00:00:35.341
Re-estimated horizons mostly stayed within earlier confidence intervals, but the trend steepened: the doubling time since 2023 came out at about 131 days (roughly 4.

7
00:00:35.621 --> 00:00:43.003
3 months), versus about 165 days on the old suite, and about 89 days since 2024.

8
00:00:43.283 --> 00:00:59.368
METR flagged the caveats in the same post: few tasks remain that the newest models fail, only 5 of the 31 tasks estimated at 8 hours or more have measured human baselines, and the infrastructure change itself shifted some models’ scores.

9
00:00:59.648 --> 00:01:00.501
The ceiling.

10
00:01:00.781 --> 00:01:14.968
METR’s live time-horizons page added a warning that measurements above about 16 hours are unreliable with the current suite, and on 8 September 2026 it marked the page as no longer actively updated.

11
00:01:15.248 --> 00:01:20.859
In other words, the best-known capability ruler ran out of length during 2026.

12
00:01:21.139 --> 00:01:29.267
That is itself evidence of fast progress, and it also means that recent “horizon” numbers you see quoted are poorly constrained.

13
00:01:29.547 --> 00:01:33.173
The Frontier Risk Report (19 May 2026).

14
00:01:33.453 --> 00:01:53.187
Covering February and March 2026, with access to Anthropic, Google, Meta, and OpenAI, METR estimated the public frontier’s 50% horizon at about 12 hours (with a very wide range, roughly 5 to 61 hours) and judged internal models likely at least 16 hours.

15
00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:56.880
Its 80% estimate for public models was about 1.

16
00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:58.184
5 hours.

17
00:01:58.464 --> 00:02:12.416
And it reported something every benchmark reader should remember: on review, at least 16% of “successful” runs on tasks longer than 8 hours were illegitimate, meaning the agent cheated rather than solved the task.
